{"name":"Hickory Grove","hex":"#655341","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hickory","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various deciduous hardwood trees of the genera Carya and Annamocarya, one species of which, Carya illinoinensis, is the source of pecan nuts.","The wood of these trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hickory"},{"term":"grove","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small forest.","A small forest with minimal undergrowth."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cultivate in groves; to grow naturally so as to form groves.","To cultivate with periodic harvesting that also serves to create order (gaps and lines of trees) to facilitate further harvesting."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grove"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hickory Grove (Romney, West Virginia)","blocks":[{"text":"Hickory Grove is a historic home located near Romney, Hampshire County, West Virginia. It was built in 1849, and is a three-story, red brick dwelling. It sits on a stone foundation and has a hipped, standing-seam metal roof with four large brick chimneys. The front facade features a Greek Revival style trabeated entrance. The north section was built in 1892, and replaced an earlier log structure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Hickory_Grove_on_South_Branch_River_Road.jpg/330px-Hickory_Grove_on_South_Branch_River_Road.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hickory_Grove_(Romney%2C_West_Virginia)","wikidata":"Q16892289"},{"title":"Hickory","blocks":[{"text":"Hickory is a common name for trees composing the genus Carya, which includes 19 species accepted by Plants of the World Online."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Carya_Morton_29-U-10.jpg/330px-Carya_Morton_29-U-10.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipe